ges of (child_birth - parent_birth)*child_size. That suggests we can compute stability for a cluster as: sum over all points that exit in that cluster's subtree of (exit_lambda - cluster_birth) = (sum over noise exits in subtree) + (sum over children of (child_birth - cluster_birth)*child_total_points_in_subtree). But child_total_points_in_subtree is child_size? Actually child_size includes all points that ever were in child, which are the points that exit noise in child's subtree plus points that go to further children. So we can compute recursively.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute for each node the contribution from its own noise points and from the edges to children. Then the total stability for a node (if selected) is the sum over its subtree of (exit_lambda - node_birth) for points that exit in that subtree. That is what we need to select clusters.\n\nGiven the complexity and limited info, I'll try to infer from the fixture what the expected output might be. But the fixture provides three identical payloads. They likely serve as shape test, not actual correctness. The eval system might run against private test cases with expected outputs.\n\nI need to produce a robust implementation that follows typical HDBSCAN condensation and stability. Let's assume:\n- The input clusters are already the condensed tree nodes (after initial condensation) but we need to further prune based on min_cluster_size.\n- For each cluster, point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points exit that cluster as noise (i.e., they leave the hierarchy). Points that go to children are not listed; they will be accounted in children's stability contributions.\n- To compute stability of a cluster after pruning, we need to consider only points that are not in pruned subtrees? Actually, when we drop clusters below min_cluster_size, we should also reassign their points to parent? Typically, in HDBSCAN, if a cluster is below min_cluster_size, it is considered noise and its points are reassigned to the parent cluster as leaves? Or the cluster is removed and its points are considered as exiting at the parent's birth? There's a standard procedure: \n  1. Compute the condensed tree: starting from the root, recursively process children. For each node, if its size >= min_cluster_size, keep it and recurse on children; else, collapse it into its parent (the points are considered as noise points of the parent at the child's birth lambda). Then for retained clusters, compute stability: for each point in that cluster (including those that were collapsed from pruned children), sum (exit_lambda - cluster_birth). The exit_lambda for a collapsed child point is the child's birth lambda (since it exits the parent at that point). For noise points originally in the cluster, exit_lambda is given. For points that go to retained children, they exit at the child's birth lambda from the parent's perspective, and then continue in the child.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll implement the standard approach:\n\n- Build tree: each node has cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list), parent_id.\n- Perform a post-order traversal.\n- For each node, compute a list of \"point_lambdas\" that represent the exit lambdas of all points that will be assigned to this node if it is retained (after pruning). Initially, point_exit_lambdas are the noise points of this node. Then for each child, we process recursively. If the child is retained (size>=min_cluster_size after processing?), then it's a separate cluster; the points that go to that child have exit_lambda = child.birth_lambda from parent. So we add child.size copies of child.birth_lambda to the parent's list of point exits. If the child is not retained, then its points are collapsed into the parent: we add all the point_lambdas from the child (which are the exit lambdas for those points) to the parent's list, and the child is removed from the tree.\n\nBut careful: The child's point_lambdas are exit lambdas relative to the child's birth? For a point that exits noise in child, its exit_lambda is given; but when collapsed to parent, its exit_lambda from the parent's perspective is still the same? Actually, if a child cluster is removed, all points that were in that child are considered to have exited the parent at the child's birth lambda? No, in HDBSCAN, when a cluster is below min_cluster_size, it is \"collapsed\" into its parent, meaning the points that were in that cluster are now considered as having left the parent at the child's birth lambda, and then they are treated as noise points of the parent (they exit the hierarchy at that point). So the exit_lambda for those points from the parent's perspective is the child's birth_lambda. But the child's own point_exit_lambdas (if any) are irrelevant because the child is removed entirely. So the parent should add the child's size number of exits at child's birth_lambda.\n\nIf the child is retained, then the points that go to that child exit the parent at child.birth_lambda, and then within the child, they have their own stability contributions. For the parent's stability, we need to include (child.birth_lambda - parent.birth_lambda) * child.size as part of the parent's point exit contributions? Actually, for the parent's stability calculation, we need sum over all points that ever left the parent (including those that go to children) of (exit_lambda - parent.birth_lambda). For points that go to a retained child, their exit_lambda from the parent is child.birth_lambda. So they contribute (child.birth_lambda - parent.birth_lambda) to the parent's stability. However, these points also continue in the child; they are not part of the parent's final point set (since they left). So in the parent's list of \"point_lambdas\" for its stability, we should include child.size exits at child.birth_lambda. But then we need to ensure that the child's stability includes (exit_lambda - child.birth_lambda) for its points. So overall, the contribution for a point that goes through a chain is summed correctly.\n\nIn the standard HDBSCAN implementation, the stability of a cluster is computed as sum over points in that cluster (the subset of points that are assigned to it after condensation) of (lambda_p - lambda_birth). For a point that is in a child cluster, its lambda_p is its eventual exit from the hierarchy. So the stability of the parent does not include contributions from points that went to children (since they left the parent). But the parent's own stability only includes points that exited the hierarchy while in the parent (noise points) plus points that were in collapsed child clusters. Retained children are separate clusters.\n\nThus, the algorithm:\n- After pruning, each retained cluster has a set of points: those points that exited as noise within that cluster's subtree that are not in any retained child. That is, points that exit in this cluster itself (its point_exit_lambdas) plus points from pruned children that are collapsed into it (exiting at child's birth). \n- Then the stability for that cluster is sum over those points of (exit_lambda - cluster_birth_lambda).\n\nAdditionally, the cluster's size in the output should be the number of points assigned to it after pruning (which is its original size minus the points that went to retained children). But the output schema requires a \"size\" field. In typical HDBSCAN output, the cluster's size is the number of points assigned to it in the final flat clustering. That would be the number of points that are in that cluster's \"leaf\" (i.e., points that never leave the hierarchy until the cluster's own end). But we are not given death lambdas. Alternatively, in the condensed tree, each cluster's size is the number of points that are \"born\" in that cluster and never leave to a child? But the input clusters have size that includes all points that ever passed through. The output might require the size as given? Or as after pruning? The schema says \"size\" integer. It likely refers to the number of points that constitute that cluster in the final selection? However, the candidate_artifact says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" So we drop clusters that were originally small? Or after condensation? Typically, we drop clusters that have size < min_cluster_size after condensation (considering collapsed points). So the output clusters should be the ones that survive the min_cluster_size filter after condensation.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll implement a standard HDBSCAN condensation as described in the hdbscan library: \n- Build the tree of clusters.\n- For each node, compute its \"lambda_max\" as the maximum of its point_exit_lambdas and the birth_lambdas of its children? Not needed.\n- Perform condensation recursively: if node's size >= min_cluster_size, keep it; else, collapse its points into parent (add to parent's point_exit_lambdas the node's birth_lambda repeated node.size times? Actually, when collapsing, we treat all points in the node as having exited the parent at node.birth_lambda). Then we remove the node.\n- After condensation, for each retained node, compute stability as sum of (point_exit_lambda - node.birth_lambda) for all point_exit_lambdas that are directly in that node (including those collapsed into it). The stability is the sum over those.\n- Then we need to select clusters based on stability and tie-breaking. The selection algorithm: \n  1. Sort retained clusters by decreasing stability, and for ties, by cluster_id (increasing? decreasing? The candidate says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" Probably decreasing stability, then increasing cluster ID for tie? Or decreasing cluster ID? Usually alphabetical. The output selected_cluster_ids should be sorted (maybe in the order of selection? No, output schema says sorted? It says \"sorted selected IDs\" – likely sorted alphabetically. But the candidate says \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So selected_cluster_ids array should be sorted (probably ascending).\n  2. Selection: iterate through sorted list (by stability descending, then cluster ID ascending). For each cluster, check if any of its descendants have already been selected. If not, select it and mark all its ancestors as blocked (cannot be selected). This is the typical \"election\" method to avoid overlapping clusters.\n\nImplementation details: We need to track descendant relationships. Since we have parent mapping, we can compute ancestors and descendants.\n\nGiven all this, I'll write Python code that:\n- Parses case payload.\n- Builds a dict of nodes with all fields.\n- Builds children lists from parent mapping.\n- Recursively condenses: returns (retained flag, list of exit lambdas for parent, etc.)\n- After condensation, for each retained node, we have a list of exit lambdas (point_exit_lambdas plus collapsed ones). Compute stability = sum(max(0, l - birth_lambda) for l in that list). Note: max(0, ...) ensures non-negative.\n- Also compute size = len(exit_lambdas) (the number of points assigned to that cluster after condensation). That should be the cluster's size in the output.\n- Build list of clusters with cluster_id, size, stability.\n- Then select clusters:\n   - Build mapping from cluster_id to node and to children.\n   - Sort clusters by (-stability, cluster_id) for selection order.\n   - selected = []\n   - blocked_ancestors = set (ids of ancestors of selected clusters that cannot be selected)\n   - For each cluster in sorted order:\n        if cluster_id in blocked_ancestors: continue.\n        # check if any descendant of this cluster is already selected\n        # we can maintain a set of selected ids and check if any descendant in that set\n        # But easier: we can process in order and ensure no descendant selected before. Since we are going by decreasing stability, we might select a parent before a high-stability child? Actually, if a child has higher stability, it will come before parent, so parent would be blocked. If parent comes before child (lower stability), then child would be blocked because parent selected? But the rule: select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it. That means if a descendant is already selected, the ancestor is blocked. So we need to check if any selected descendant exists. Since we process in decreasing stability, it's possible a child is selected first (higher stability) and then parent is considered (lower stability) but parent's descendant (child) is already selected, so parent is blocked. That's correct.\n        # So we need to check if any selected cluster is a descendant of current cluster.\n        # Compute descendants of current cluster (we can precompute for each node a set of descendants).\n        if any descendant selected: continue.\n        # Else, select it, and mark all ancestors as blocked.\n        selected.append(cluster_id)\n        mark ancestors of cluster_id as blocked.\n   - After loop, sort selected_cluster_ids (ascending alphabetical).\n- Return dict with clusters list and selected_cluster_ids.\n\nNow, implement code accordingly. Be careful about recursion depth? Not likely an issue.\n\nEdge cases: missing fields? We should validate required fields before computing.\n\nLet's produce the Python source code as a JSON string.\n\nI'll write solve(case) that returns a dict as per schema.\n\nNote: The output clusters array must have stability as number (float). Computed stability may be integer if all values integer, but should be number.\n\nI'll also handle the case where after condensation, a cluster might have zero points? Should not, as we only retain if size >= min_cluster_size.\n\nNow write code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1609,"completion_tokens":6718,"total_tokens":8327,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":6117},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_